-- title:
--   Theorem 2 — every zonotope is $(1\pm\varepsilon)$-approximated by a zonotope with $O(n/\varepsilon^2)$ segments
-- statement:
--   There is an absolute constant $C>0$ such that the following holds. Let $Z\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ be a zonotope, that is, $Z=\{y^\top A:\ y\in[-1,1]^m\}=\sum_{i=1}^m[-a_i,a_i]$ for some matrix $A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$ with rows $a_1,\dots,a_m$ (any $m$, any rank, zero rows allowed), and let $0<\varepsilon\le1$. Then there is a zonotope $Z'=\sum_{j=1}^k[-b_j,b_j]$ generated by $k\le C\,n/\varepsilon^2$ segments, with arbitrary $b_1,\dots,b_k\in\mathbb R^n$, such that
--   $$(1-\varepsilon)Z\subseteq Z'\subseteq(1+\varepsilon)Z .$$
--
--   This is the geometric form of the main result: through support functions it says that the $\ell_1$-type norm $u\mapsto\sum_i|\langle a_i,u\rangle|$ is approximated within $1\pm\varepsilon$ by a sum of $O(n/\varepsilon^2)$ such terms. Unlike Theorem 1, the new generators need not be rescaled rows of $A$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a zonotope Z ⊆ ℝⁿ (resp. Z′ generated by k segments) → zonotope A 1 for A ∈ ℝ^{m×n} (resp. zonotope B 1 for B ∈ ℝ^{k×n}); reason: the paper's definition on p.2: a zonotope is {yᵀA : y ∈ [−1,1]^m}, the Minkowski sum of the segments [−aᵢ, aᵢ]
--   - encoding: at most O(n/ε²) segments → k ≤ C·n/ε² for one C > 0 chosen before m, n, A, ε; reason: O(·) with an absolute constant means one uniform C
-- source:
--   Victor Reis, Thomas Rothvoss, Linear-size ℓ1 sparsifiers, STOC 2027 submission #162 (October 2026; PDF SHA-256 5137e7dee3cd303bd508f90f5f42c955632af5dc2ec978d9ef575bae4ce23710), §1.1, Theorem 2, p.3; zonotope definition p.2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_L1Sparsifiers_Main_Zonotope

namespace L1Sparsifiers.Main.Supplement

open scoped Pointwise

/-- **Theorem 2** (Reis–Rothvoss, *Linear-size ℓ1 sparsifiers*, STOC 2027 submission #162, p.3).
"For any zonotope Z ⊆ ℝⁿ and any 0 < ε ≤ 1, there exists a zonotope Z′ generated by at most
O(n/ε²) segments so that (1 − ε)Z ⊆ Z′ ⊆ (1 + ε)Z."

A zonotope is origin-centred (p.2): the zonotope generated by `A ∈ ℝ^{m×n}` is
`Z = {yᵀA : y ∈ [−1,1]^m} = zonotope A 1`, for any number `m` of rows, any rank, zero rows allowed.
`Z′ = zonotope B 1` is generated by the `k` rows of an arbitrary `B ∈ ℝ^{k×n}` (its generators need
not be rescaled rows of `A`). The `O(n/ε²)` is one absolute constant `C > 0`, chosen before
`m, n, A, ε`. -/
theorem theorem_2 :
    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧
      ∀ (m n : ℕ) (A : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin n) ℝ) (ε : ℝ), 0 < ε → ε ≤ 1 →
        ∃ (k : ℕ) (B : Matrix (Fin k) (Fin n) ℝ), (k : ℝ) ≤ C * n / ε ^ 2 ∧
          (1 - ε) • zonotope A 1 ⊆ zonotope B 1 ∧
          zonotope B 1 ⊆ (1 + ε) • zonotope A 1 := by sorry

end L1Sparsifiers.Main.Supplement
